  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1140 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA cutoff low pressure system continues lumbering northeastward\nand interacts with another vigorous southward-moving area of\nvorticity from central Canada today through Friday. As a cold\nfront and associated dry slot swings through this afternoon, 850\nmb temperatures drop from the low teens to the single digits\nbelow zero Friday and Friday night indicating cooler air\nentering the region. Temperatures fall back into the 30s\nThursday night with lower 40s in NW IN where extra lake effect\nclouds will be possible. Then, lows fall back into the upper 20s\nto mid 30s across NW IL Friday night and similar temperatures \nwill also be possible Saturday night. Additionally, gusts\nbetween 20 and 30 mph decrease this evening, but a few sporadic\n20 to 25 mph gusts cannot be ruled out overnight. Gusts between\n20 and 25 mph could still occur on Thursday as a trough drops\ndown within the north and western side of the low. Some wrap\naround spotty showers and drizzle are likely along and out in \nfront of this disturbance. This large area of resultant \nvorticity now slides southeastward as a surface high slides \nthrough overhead into the south central CONUS. Then, as the \ndisturbance and cold air move Le MI, lake effect showers will \nbe possible over NW IN and over Le MI. High pressure follows \nbehind and switches trajectories around decreasing instability, \nand dry air comes in. This allows high temperatures to warm back\nup through the weekend in the 50s to the 60s for next week. \nLows warm through the 30s around the middle of next week. \n\nNorthwest flow aloft takes over into next week as ridging ins\nentrenched across the southwestern CONUS. This keeps the \nforecast area dry through the rest of the forecast period as \nenergetic waves stay to the north near the border with Canada. \n\nRoller",
